\begin{align*}
f(x)+f\left(1 - \frac{1}{x}\right) &= \log|x|,\\
f\left(1 - \frac{1}{x}\right) &= \log|x| - f(x) ,\\
f\left(1 - \frac{1}{x}\right) &= \log\left|\frac{x}{x-1}\right| , \qquad \boxed{\text{for } x \neq 1}.
\end{align*}
Setting $x=2007$ into this equation gives $f(1- \frac{1}{2007}) = \log(2007) - f(2007)$.
In addition, setting $x=1- \frac{1}{2007}$ gives $f(2006) = \log 2007-\log(2007-1) = \log 2007-\log 2006$, so
\begin{align*}
f(1-\frac{1}{2007}) &= \log\left|\frac{2007}{2006}\right| \\
&= \log(2007) - \log(2006) = \log 2007 - \log (domain value), \quad \text{so that } \log 2007 = f(2006) .
\end{align*}
Thus, \[ \log 2007 = f(1 - \frac{1}{2007}) = \log(2007) - f(2007),\] which yields $f(2007) = f(1 - \frac{1}{2007}) = \log (2007) - \log(2007) = \boxed{\log (2007)}$.
The answer is: \log(2007)

