Denote the six vertices of the hexagon with $z_k = e^{\pi i k/3}$ for $k = 0, 1,...5$.  Let $w_k$ be the sixth root of $z_k^{-3}$, where $z_k$ is defined as above:
\begin{align*}
w_k &= \frac 1 {z_k^3} = \frac 1 {e^{\pi i k/2}} = e^{-\pi i k/2} = e^{\pi i(-(k + 1)/2)}.
\end{align*}

So $S = \left\lbrace \frac 1 {z_k} \ | \ k = 0, 1,...,5 \right\rbrace$ is the same as $\text{${w}_0, \text{${w}_0}, \text{${w}_2}, \text{${w}_2}, \text{${w}_4}, \text{${w}_4}$}$}$. This is just the closed unit disk.  Together with the remaining vertices of the hexagon, we have $16$ vertices.  The region $S$ is thus a circle of radius one.  We can compute the area of $S$ by subtracting the area of the regular hexagon from a circle of radius one.  The area of the circle is $\pi \cdot 1^2 = \pi$, and the area of the hexagon can be computed as $(3/2)\sqrt3 = \frac{3\sqrt3}2$.  The desired area is $\pi - \frac{3\sqrt3}2.$ The answer is \pi-\frac{3\sqrt3}2

